course: dictionary
lesson: 538
title: Old words: athwart
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words are old. You will not hear them in the playground, but you will meet them in old stories and sea writing. Athwart is one of them. It means across, from one side of something to the other. «The fallen tree lay athwart the path.» The tree lies across the path, from one side to the other.
explain: Sailors use athwart too. On a ship, it means across the ship, from side to side. Athwartships means the same thing: across the ship. So if you read a sea story and see athwart, picture a line from one side of the boat to the other.
explain: Use the clues around the word. «A beam of sunlight fell athwart the room.» A beam of light cannot fall round a room. It crosses it, from wall to wall. Now try the swap check: put across in place of athwart, and the sentence still makes sense.
fact: Athwart means across, from one side to the other.
fact: Athwart is an old or literary word, so you will mostly find it in old books and sea writing.
fact: On a ship, athwart means from side to side, and athwartships means across the ship.
fact: Athwart can also mean against or opposite to, but this is an old use that you will rarely meet.
fact: Old words like athwart appear in The Wind in the Willows, in the sea talk and river scenes.
exercise: «The fallen tree lay athwart the path.» What does athwart mean here? | Under the path and out of sight | Along the path, running with it | *Across, from one side to the other | Far away from the path
exercise: On a ship, what does athwartships mean? | Down into the water | *Across the ship, from side to side | Towards the front of the boat | Up the mast to the top
exercise: «A beam of sunlight fell athwart the room.» Which word is the best swap for athwart? | around the edge of | inside | *across | under
test: «The old map showed a road athwart the hills.» What does athwart mean? | Behind the hills, hidden from view | Round and round the hills in a circle | Up the hills to the very top | *Across the hills, from one side to the other
test: Sam says athwart is a new word for a fast car. Is he right? | *No, athwart is an old word meaning across | Yes, it means very fast | No, it means dark and quiet | Yes, it is a word for a loud engine
exam: «A beam of sunlight fell athwart the room.» What does athwart mean in this sentence? | Quickly and quietly | *Across the room, from one side to the other | Sleeping in a corner | Above the ceiling
